PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 6’s proposal to parse the terminal B–G descent as two successive 9/8 steps is the one point that still feels under-motivated. The melodic contour at “geschwind” is unambiguously a descending minor third in the Frömmel source, exactly the same interval class already realized as 6/5 earlier in the verse (G–E). Inserting an extra pair of major seconds would require an ad-hoc change of interval table that has no precedent in any preceding step and would also contradict the path-dependent rule we have all been applying consistently.  

Once that single 6/5 assignment is retained, the exhaustive product already tallied by several colleagues collapses by one further factor of 2, leaving the reduced ratio 80/81. With the opening G fixed at 392 Hz the terminal frequency is therefore 392 × 80/81 = 31360/81 Hz. The same figure emerges whether one works from the A-phrase alone or from the full stanza; the only variability left is the conventional octave placement, which does not alter the coprime pair [31360,81].  

This reading stays inside the five-limit table used throughout, preserves every interval assignment already verified, and avoids any extra 3-factors in the denominator that would appear only if the minor-third reading were abandoned.}

